{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788370795,"nonce":"nVnzSYaG6SL3SQ5jbpeYOu0w2Ng=","title":"dialog-accept takes the game's generated name and nothing else: the agent cannot name its own colony","message":"`dialog-accept` (shipped 2026-09-02, git-bug `5cb1f9f`) answers a `Dialog_GiveName` by accepting the value the window already holds — the name `NameGenerator.GenerateName(Faction.OfPlayer.def.factionNameMaker, IsValidName)` produced in the dialog's constructor. That is the minimal un-wedging act and it is deliberately all it does: the advance loop sweeps naming dialogs automatically, so an unattended run passes day 4.3 without a human, and the colony is called whatever the game rolled.\n\n**The agent has no way to choose.** `name` / `second_name` are REFUSED by `VerbArgs.RefuseStray` rather than ignored, precisely so a caller that thinks it named its colony cannot silently get the generated name instead. So the refusal is honest, and the capability is missing.\n\nThis is a play decision, not an un-wedging, which is why it was split out. A colony's name is the one piece of identity an agent-run colony carries into its own transcript, its run report and `RUNS/\u003cid\u003e/`, and it currently reads as a random world-gen artifact.\n\n## What it needs\n\n`dialog-accept {name, second_name}` — or `{fields: {...}}` if a generic text-entry write turns out to be the right shape once a second text dialog exists — routed through the window's OWN validators, in the widget's own order:\n\n    if (IsValidName(text2) \u0026\u0026 (!useSecondName || IsValidSecondName(text3)))\n\n`RimWorld/Dialog_GiveName.DoWindowContents`, OK branch. Both validators are already bound and already called by `PawnActs.AcceptNameDialog`; supplying text is a matter of substituting the argument before the `IsValidName` call rather than reading `curName`. A rejected name comes back `ok:false` with the game's own `invalidNameMessageKey` translated — `PlayerFactionNameIsInvalid` / `PlayerFactionBaseNameIsInvalid` — never silently.\n\nThe validators are strict and the refusals must name which clause failed: `NamePlayerFactionDialogUtility.IsValidName` requires non-empty, \u003c= 64 chars, `GenText.IsValidFilename(s)` — **the faction name becomes a filename under permadeath** — and `!GrammarResolver.ContainsSpecialChars(s)`. `NamePlayerSettlementDialogUtility.IsValidName` drops the filename clause and keeps the rest.\n\nAlso decide the AUTO path's behaviour, which is the real design question: the sweep in `TimeDriver.SweepNameDialogs` runs before the halt, so a name the agent wants to choose is accepted out from under it. Options, in the order they should be evaluated: (a) the sweep halts ONCE on the first naming dialog of a colony and only auto-accepts on the re-raise, giving the agent exactly one turn to name it; (b) the loop names the colony proactively, before day 4.3, via a verb that opens nothing (`NamePlayerFactionDialogUtility.Named` is public static and takes a string); (c) a config/turn-level opt-in. (b) is the one that costs no turns and needs no dialog at all — worth checking whether naming the faction early suppresses the dialog outright, since `CanNameFaction` tests `!Faction.OfPlayer.HasName`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. `dialog-accept {name:\"\u003cchosen\u003e\"}` on an open `Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement` sets `Faction.OfPlayer.Name` to the chosen value, and `{second_name}` sets the settlement's, each verified by reading it back from `digest`/`interactions` rather than from the verb's own echo.\n2. An invalid name is refused with `ok:false`, the game's own translated `invalidNameMessageKey`, and a `rejected` field naming which of the two was bad — and NOTHING is named: a valid first name must not be applied when the second is rejected, because `DoWindowContents` evaluates both before naming either.\n3. A name failing only `GenText.IsValidFilename` is refused with that clause named, and the refusal says why it matters (permadeath renames the save file).\n4. The interaction with the auto-sweep is DECIDED and written into `DESIGN.md`'s decisions log and `checklists/triggered.md`'s `colony-naming` item, not left to whichever fires first.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-name-choice.md` — a numbered command list for the orchestrator's bench, not a Python harness (session 22's lesson).","files":null}]}